WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.865
Separate three questions when software accesses memory.

2
00:00:04.865 --> 00:00:08.756
A cache holds reusable instructions or data,

3
00:00:08.756 --> 00:00:13.709
helping avoid repeated accesses to more distant storage.

4
00:00:13.709 --> 00:00:21.139
In a paged system, the MMU uses page tables to translate virtual addresses and check

5
00:00:21.139 --> 00:00:22.643
page permissions.

6
00:00:22.643 --> 00:00:29.541
PMP controls read, write and execute permissions for physical-address regions;

7
00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:34.406
its effect also depends on privilege mode and settings.

8
00:00:34.406 --> 00:00:40.420
Our RV64, Sv39 source example includes an MMU and eight PMP entries.

9
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.781
Those describe different capabilities.

10
00:00:43.781 --> 00:00:48.734
Reusing a value may avoid another distant-memory access,

11
00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:53.688
while mapping and permissions remain separate questions.

12
00:00:53.688 --> 00:00:58.906
A cache hit does not establish that an access is permitted.

13
00:00:58.906 --> 00:01:03.240
The parallel comparison teaches responsibilities,

14
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.839
rather than three fixed serial hardware checkpoints.

15
00:01:07.839 --> 00:01:15.446
Their presence and integration must still be checked in the selected configuration and

16
00:01:15.446 --> 00:01:16.065
system.

17
00:01:16.065 --> 00:01:20.841
Sv39 is one RV64 paging mode; Sv32 is an RV32 example,

18
00:01:20.841 --> 00:01:24.909
while Bare does not perform paged translation.

19
00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.659
PMP needs effective-privilege, entry-matching and setting checks.

20
00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:35.700
Its implementation count is not a conformance conclusion.

